{"name":"Pale Mountain Lake Turquoise","hex":"#bae1d3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pale","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Light in color.","Having a pallor (a light color, especially due to sickness, shock, fright etc.)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To turn pale; to lose colour.","To become insignificant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pale"},{"term":"mountain","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An elevation of land of considerable dimensions rising more or less abruptly, forming a conspicuous figure in the landscape, usually having a small extent of surface at its summit. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Something very large in size or quantity; a huge amount; a great heap."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mountain"},{"term":"lake","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large, landlocked stretch of water or similar liquid.","A large amount of liquid."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An offering, sacrifice, gift.","Play; sport; game; fun; glee."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lake"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mountain","blocks":[{"text":"A mountain is an elevated portion of the surface of a planet, generally with steep sides that show significant exposed bedrock. Although there are no universally accepted definitions, a mountain is usually considered higher than a hill. By definition, mountains are often thought of as being a landform which is higher than 2,000 feet (610 m). It may either have a limited summit area or be a smaller plateau with high elevation and steep sides. A few mountains are isolated summits, but most occur in mountain ranges."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg/330px-Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain","wikidata":"Q8502"},{"title":"Lake","blocks":[{"text":"A lake is often a naturally occurring, relatively large and fixed body of water on or near the Earth's surface. It is localized in a basin or interconnected basins surrounded by dry land. Lakes lie completely on land and are separate from the ocean, although they may be connected with the ocean by outflowing rivers. Lakes, like other bodies of water, are part of the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Most lakes are fresh water and account for almost all the world's surface freshwater, but some are salt lakes with salinities even higher than that of seawater. Lakes vary significantly in surface area and volume of water, but in total cover approximately 2.5 × 106 km2 (less than 2%) of the Earth's surface."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Lake_Idro_Italy_2005-08-16.jpg/330px-Lake_Idro_Italy_2005-08-16.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake","wikidata":"Q23397"},{"title":"Turquoise","blocks":[{"text":"Turquoise is an opaque, blue-to-green mineral that is a hydrous phosphate of copper and aluminium, with the chemical formula CuAl6(PO4)4(OH)8·4H2O. It is rare and valuable in finer grades and has been prized as a gemstone for millennia due to its hue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Turquoise-40031.jpg/330px-Turquoise-40031.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turquoise","wikidata":"Q165254"},{"title":"Turquoise (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Turquoise is a cyan color, based on the mineral of the same name."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turquoise_(color)","wikidata":"Q5960345"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-V